module dev.slatelang.slate

// Regular expressions, as JavaScript.
//
// **`RegExp` is not PCRE2, and the whole of this file is the difference between them.** slate's
// patterns are Perl's, which is what `regex.sysl` gets from `sh.sysl.pcre2`; a browser has one
// regular expression engine and it is `RegExp`. So a pattern is TRANSLATED on its way in rather
// than handed over, and where a translation would be a guess the pattern is refused NAMING THE
// CONSTRUCT -- which is the rule the whole back end is built on, said for one library.
//
// **The three ways a construct can differ, and what each one gets.** A construct spelled
// differently and meaning the same thing is TRANSLATED: `(?P<n>...)` is `(?<n>...)`, `\z` is
// `(?![\s\S])`, `[[:alpha:]]` is `[A-Za-z]`. A construct `RegExp` does not have at all is REFUSED:
// an atomic group, a possessive quantifier, recursion, `\K`. **And a construct both engines accept
// while MEANING DIFFERENT THINGS is refused too, and it is the dangerous kind** -- `\s` is the one
// that matters and it is measured below.
//
// **The `u` flag is always set, and that decision buys and costs.** Without it `RegExp` counts a
// string in UTF-16 code units, so `.` matches half of an emoji and `^.$` says an astral character
// is two; PCRE2 is in UTF mode and counts characters, so nothing else agrees. With it `RegExp` also
// gets stricter -- `[\d-x]`, a range with a class at one end, is refused by `u` exactly as PCRE2
// refuses it, which closes one of the two places `RegExp` was the more permissive engine for free.
// What it costs is that a few PCRE2 spellings become syntax errors rather than literals, so the
// translator writes them out: a lone `{`, a lone `]`, `\%` and its family.
//
// **The measurements that decided the translations**, taken through this project's own PCRE2 and
// through node, both at 2026-09-04:
//
//   - **`\s` IS NOT `\s`.** PCRE2 here is not in UCP mode, so `\s` is the six ASCII spaces;
//     `RegExp` reads it as every Unicode space, twenty-five of them. A pattern using `\s` to split
//     fields would quietly cut on a non-breaking space in a browser and not in the interpreter.
//     `\s`, `\S` and their in-class forms are written out.
//   - **`.` IS NOT `.`.** PCRE2's excludes the newline; `RegExp`'s also excludes `\r`, ` ` and
//     ` `. So `.` becomes `[^\n]`, and `[\s\S]` where `s` was asked for.
//   - **`^` AND `$` UNDER `m` ARE NOT `^` AND `$` UNDER `m`**, for the same reason and in the same
//     three characters. They become lookarounds, and `RegExp`'s own `m` is never set.
//   - **A lookbehind is where `RegExp` is the LOOSER engine.** PCRE2 refuses one whose length is
//     unlimited -- `(?<=ab*)` is "length of lookbehind assertion is not limited" -- and takes a
//     bounded one, `(?<=ab?)` and `(?<=a{2,3})` both compiling. `RegExp` takes all of them. So an
//     unbounded quantifier inside a lookbehind is refused HERE, and the two engines agree again.
//   - **`\w` under `i` is left alone and is the one difference that is documented rather than
//     closed.** `RegExp` with `u` and `i` folds `U+017F` and `U+212A` into `s` and `k`, so it counts
//     them as word characters where PCRE2 does not. It is exactly two code points and only under
//     `i`; closing it would mean rewriting every character class through the `v` flag's set
//     subtraction, which is a much newer thing to require of a browser than the difference is worth.
//     `docs/reference/javascript.md` names them.
//
// **The two halves of this file sit in different scopes**, as `js_rt_time.sysl`'s do: the value
// model and the translator are script-level, and the builtins are in `js_rt_regex_builtins.sysl`
// inside the `$b` block.

// The value model, the table of compiled patterns, and the translator.
js_runtime_regex() -> string = raw"""
    // -- the value ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

    // A compiled pattern. **A SLOT and not the `RegExp` itself**, which is `value.sysl`'s
    // `RegexVal` and is what makes two identically written patterns equal: the table is keyed by
    // the source and the flags, so `regex("a") == regex("a")` without comparing any text.
    class SRegex {
        constructor(id) { this.id = id }
    }

    // What a slot holds: what the program wrote, and what node was given.
    const REGEX_TABLE = []
    const REGEX_IDS = new Map()

    // The six ASCII spaces PCRE2 calls `\s`, and everything else. **Both are needed because a class
    // cannot be negated from inside another one**: `[^...]` is the whole class or nothing, so
    // `[\S]` has to be written as the ranges that are not `\s` rather than as a negation.
    const RE_SPACE_IN = "\\t\\n\\x0B\\f\\r "
    const RE_SPACE_OUT = "\\x00-\\x08\\x0E-\\x1F\\x21-\\u{10FFFF}"

    // PCRE2's horizontal and vertical whitespace, measured rather than read off a manual: `\h` is
    // the tab, the space, the no-break space, the ogham space mark, the Mongolian vowel separator,
    // the six-per-em run, the narrow and medium mathematical spaces and the ideographic space;
    // `\v` is the four ASCII line breaks, the next-line character and the two Unicode separators.
    const RE_HSPACE_IN = "\\t \\xA0\\u1680\\u180E\\u2000-\\u200A\\u202F\\u205F\\u3000"
    const RE_HSPACE_OUT = "\\x00-\\x08\\x0A-\\x1F\\x21-\\x9F\\xA1-\\u167F\\u1681-\\u180D" +
        "\\u180F-\\u1FFF\\u200B-\\u202E\\u2030-\\u205E\\u2060-\\u2FFF\\u3001-\\u{10FFFF}"
    const RE_VSPACE_IN = "\\n\\x0B\\f\\r\\x85\\u2028\\u2029"
    const RE_VSPACE_OUT = "\\x00-\\x09\\x0E-\\x84\\x86-\\u2027\\u202A-\\u{10FFFF}"

    // A line break as one thing, which is what `\R` is for: a CRLF pair counts once.
    const RE_ANYCRLF = "(?:\\r\\n|[" + RE_VSPACE_IN + "])"

    // The POSIX classes, each measured over the whole of ASCII through this project's own PCRE2.
    // **They are ASCII and their negations are NOT**, which is the trap: `[[:^alpha:]]` matches
    // `é`, because `é` is not one of the letters `[:alpha:]` names. So a negation is written as the
    // ranges of every code point outside the set rather than as `[^...]`, which is also what lets
    // it appear beside other members -- `[x[:^digit:]]` is a class PCRE2 accepts.
    const POSIX_IN = {
        alpha: "A-Za-z",
        digit: "0-9",
        alnum: "0-9A-Za-z",
        upper: "A-Z",
        lower: "a-z",
        space: "\\t\\n\\x0B\\f\\r ",
        blank: "\\t ",
        punct: "!-\\/:-@\\[-`{-~",
        print: " -~",
        graph: "!-~",
        cntrl: "\\x00-\\x1F\\x7F",
        xdigit: "0-9A-Fa-f",
        word: "0-9A-Z_a-z",
        ascii: "\\x00-\\x7F"
    }

    const POSIX_OUT = {
        alpha: "\\x00-\\x40\\x5B-\\x60\\x7B-\\u{10FFFF}",
        digit: "\\x00-\\x2F\\x3A-\\u{10FFFF}",
        alnum: "\\x00-\\x2F\\x3A-\\x40\\x5B-\\x60\\x7B-\\u{10FFFF}",
        upper: "\\x00-\\x40\\x5B-\\u{10FFFF}",
        lower: "\\x00-\\x60\\x7B-\\u{10FFFF}",
        space: "\\x00-\\x08\\x0E-\\x1F\\x21-\\u{10FFFF}",
        blank: "\\x00-\\x08\\x0A-\\x1F\\x21-\\u{10FFFF}",
        punct: "\\x00-\\x20\\x30-\\x39\\x41-\\x5A\\x61-\\x7A\\x7F-\\u{10FFFF}",
        print: "\\x00-\\x1F\\x7F-\\u{10FFFF}",
        graph: "\\x00-\\x20\\x7F-\\u{10FFFF}",
        cntrl: "\\x20-\\x7E\\u0080-\\u{10FFFF}",
        xdigit: "\\x00-\\x2F\\x3A-\\x40\\x47-\\x60\\x67-\\u{10FFFF}",
        word: "\\x00-\\x2F\\x3A-\\x40\\x5B-\\x5E\\x60\\x7B-\\u{10FFFF}",
        ascii: "\\u0080-\\u{10FFFF}"
    }

    // The general categories `\p{...}` may name. **Anything else that is a word is taken for a
    // SCRIPT and written `\p{Script=...}`**, which is the only spelling `RegExp` has for one and
    // exactly what PCRE2 means by the bare name: `\p{Greek}` compiles here and refuses there, and
    // the two say the same thing once it is written out.
    const UNICODE_CATEGORIES = new Set(["C", "Cc", "Cf", "Cn", "Co", "Cs", "L", "Ll", "Lm", "Lo",
        "Lt", "Lu", "M", "Mc", "Me", "Mn", "N", "Nd", "Nl", "No", "P", "Pc", "Pd", "Pe", "Pf", "Pi",
        "Po", "Ps", "S", "Sc", "Sk", "Sm", "So", "Z", "Zl", "Zp", "Zs"])

    // -- the refusals ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

    // **A construct a JavaScript host does not have, refused where the pattern is written.** The
    // sentence names the construct first, because a reader holding a pattern needs to find it in
    // the text before anything else is useful.
    const reGone = (what, why) =>
        $.fault("`" + what + "` is not something a JavaScript host's regular expressions have -- " +
            why + ", so this pattern is refused here rather than quietly meaning something else")

    // **The refusal for the one construct `RegExp` DOES have, and the sentence has to be its own.**
    // A lookbehind of unlimited length is a thing a browser is perfectly happy with; what is wrong
    // with it is that PCRE2 is not, so a pattern carrying one would compile in a browser and not in
    // the interpreter. Saying "a JavaScript host does not have this" would be false and would send
    // the reader looking for a missing feature.
    const reLoose = (written) =>
        $.fault("`" + written + "` leaves this lookbehind with no limit on its length, and PCRE2 " +
            "refuses one of those -- so it is refused here too, rather than compiling in a browser " +
            "and not in the interpreter")

    // -- the translator ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

    const RE_DIGITS = "0123456789"
    const isDigitChar = (c) => c !== undefined && RE_DIGITS.indexOf(c) >= 0
    const isWordChar = (c) => c !== undefined && /[0-9A-Za-z_]/.test(c)

    // Whether what starts at `i` is a quantifier's braces -- `{2}`, `{2,}` or `{2,3}`. **PCRE2 reads
    // a `{` that is not one as a literal and `RegExp` under `u` refuses it**, so this is what tells
    // the two apart, and the literal gets written `\{`.
    const braceQuantifier = (src, i) => {
        let j = i + 1
        let low = ""
        let high = ""
        let comma = false

        while (isDigitChar(src[j])) low += src[j++]

        if (src[j] === ",") {
            comma = true
            j++

            while (isDigitChar(src[j])) high += src[j++]
        }

        if (low === "" || src[j] !== "}") return null

        return { end: j + 1, low: low, high: comma ? high : low, open: comma && high === "" }
    }

    // A code point as an escape `RegExp` accepts under `u`, whatever it is. **Used wherever a
    // PCRE2 spelling has to become a plain character** -- `\%` is `%` there and a syntax error
    // here, and writing the code point rather than the character means nothing has to know which
    // characters are special where.
    const reCodePoint = (c) => "\\u{" + c.codePointAt(0).toString(16) + "}"

    // Everything in `text` as itself. `\Q...\E`'s whole job.
    const reQuoted = (text) => {
        let out = ""

        for (const c of text) out += reCodePoint(c)

        return out
    }

    // `\p{...}` and `\P{...}`, which survive translation when `RegExp` has the name and are refused
    // when it does not. **`Any` is the one that has to be written out**, PCRE2 spelling "every code
    // point" as a property and `RegExp` not having it at all.
    const unicodeProperty = (negated, name, inClass) => {
        if (name === "Any") {
            if (negated) reGone("\\P{Any}", "a class that matches nothing is not a thing to write")

            return inClass ? "\\s\\S" : "[\\s\\S]"
        }

        if (name === "L&" || name === "Lc" || name.charAt(0) === "X")
            reGone("\\p{" + name + "}",
                "`" + name + "` is one of PCRE2's own property names rather than a Unicode one")

        const head = negated ? "\\P{" : "\\p{"

        if (UNICODE_CATEGORIES.has(name)) return head + name + "}"
        if (name.indexOf("=") >= 0) return head + name + "}"
        if (/^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z_ ]*$/.test(name)) return head + "Script=" + name + "}"

        reGone("\\p{" + name + "}", "`" + name + "` is not a property name a JavaScript host knows")
    }

    // What a pattern becomes on its way to `RegExp`. **One left-to-right pass with two states** --
    // inside a character class or not -- which is enough because every difference between the two
    // engines is local to the construct that carries it.
    //
    // `lookbehind` counts the lookbehinds this position is inside, and is what makes an unbounded
    // quantifier a refusal rather than a translation.
    const regexTranslate = (src, flags) => {
        const extended = flags.indexOf("x") >= 0
        const multiline = flags.indexOf("m") >= 0
        const dotall = flags.indexOf("s") >= 0
        const closers = []
        let out = ""
        let i = 0
        let lookbehind = 0

        while (i < src.length) {
            const c = src[i]

            // **Extended mode is stripped here rather than handed over, `RegExp` having no `x`.**
            // Whitespace and a `#` comment are ignored outside a class, which is PCRE2's rule; an
            // escaped space is not whitespace and is reached below.
            if (extended && (c === " " || c === "\t" || c === "\n" || c === "\r" || c === "\f")) {
                i++
                continue
            }

            if (extended && c === "#") {
                while (i < src.length && src[i] !== "\n") i++

                continue
            }

            if (c === "\\") {
                const r = regexEscape(src, i, false)

                out += r.text
                i = r.end
                continue
            }

            if (c === "[") {
                const r = regexClass(src, i, extended)

                out += r.text
                i = r.end
                continue
            }

            if (c === ".") {
                out += dotall ? "[\\s\\S]" : "[^\\n]"
                i++
                continue
            }

            // **`^` and `$` under `m` are written as lookarounds and `RegExp`'s own `m` is never
            // set**, because its idea of a line break is four characters and PCRE2's is one.
            if (c === "^") {
                out += multiline ? "(?:(?<![\\s\\S])|(?<=\\n))" : "^"
                i++
                continue
            }

            if (c === "$") {
                out += multiline ? "(?:(?![\\s\\S])|(?=\\n))" : "$"
                i++
                continue
            }

            if (c === "(") {
                const r = regexGroup(src, i)

                out += r.text
                i = r.end

                // **A comment and a named backreference are whole**, having eaten their own `)`, so
                // they leave nothing on the stack for a later one to close.
                if (r.opened) {
                    closers.push(r.lookbehind)

                    if (r.lookbehind) lookbehind++
                }

                continue
            }

            if (c === ")") {
                out += ")"
                i++

                if (closers.pop()) lookbehind--

                continue
            }

            if (c === "*" || c === "+" || c === "?") {
                i = regexQuantifier(src, i, i + 1, c, lookbehind, c !== "?")
                out += c + (src[i] === "?" ? "?" : "")

                if (src[i] === "?") i++

                continue
            }

            if (c === "{") {
                const q = braceQuantifier(src, i)

                if (q === null) {
                    out += "\\{"
                    i++
                    continue
                }

                // **Only an OPEN brace count is unbounded**, and that is the whole of what a
                // lookbehind refuses: PCRE2 compiles `(?<=a{2,3})` and refuses `(?<=a{2,})`.
                const written = "{" + q.low + (q.open ? "," : (q.low === q.high ? "" : "," + q.high)) + "}"

                i = regexQuantifier(src, i, q.end, written, lookbehind, q.open)
                out += written

                if (src[i] === "?") {
                    out += "?"
                    i++
                }

                continue
            }

            // **A lone `}` or `]` is a literal in PCRE2 and a syntax error under `u`**, so both are
            // written out. Everything else goes through as itself.
            if (c === "}") {
                out += "\\}"
                i++
                continue
            }

            if (c === "]") {
                out += "\\]"
                i++
                continue
            }

            out += c
            i += c.length
        }

        return out
    }

    // What follows a quantifier: `+` makes it possessive and `?` makes it lazy. **The possessive
    // form is the refusal**, `RegExp` having no way to say "and never give these back"; the lazy one
    // is left for the caller to copy. This also refuses an unbounded quantifier inside a lookbehind,
    // which is where PCRE2 is the stricter engine and the two would otherwise part company.
    const regexQuantifier = (src, at, after, written, lookbehind, unbounded) => {
        if (src[after] === "+")
            reGone(written + "+", "a possessive quantifier is PCRE2's and `RegExp` has nothing that means it")

        if (lookbehind > 0 && unbounded) reLoose(written)

        return after
    }

    // A backslash and what follows it, outside a class or inside one.
    //
    // **The in-class form of a set escape is its members and the out-of-class form is a class**,
    // which is the whole reason this takes a flag: `[\S]` cannot be written `[[^...]]`, so what goes
    // inside a class is the ranges rather than the negation.
    const regexEscape = (src, at, inClass) => {
        const c = src[at + 1]
        const two = (text) => ({ text: text, end: at + 2 })

        if (c === undefined) $.fault("this pattern ends in a backslash")

        // The sets, measured above.
        if (c === "s") return two(inClass ? RE_SPACE_IN : "[" + RE_SPACE_IN + "]")
        if (c === "S") return two(inClass ? RE_SPACE_OUT : "[" + RE_SPACE_OUT + "]")
        if (c === "h") return two(inClass ? RE_HSPACE_IN : "[" + RE_HSPACE_IN + "]")
        if (c === "H") return two(inClass ? RE_HSPACE_OUT : "[" + RE_HSPACE_OUT + "]")
        if (c === "v") return two(inClass ? RE_VSPACE_IN : "[" + RE_VSPACE_IN + "]")
        if (c === "V") return two(inClass ? RE_VSPACE_OUT : "[" + RE_VSPACE_OUT + "]")

        // **`\R` is a SEQUENCE and not a set**, a CRLF pair being one line break, so it has no
        // in-class form at all -- and PCRE2 refuses it in a class for the same reason.
        if (c === "R") {
            if (inClass) reGone("\\R in a character class", "a line break is a sequence rather than a character")

            return two(RE_ANYCRLF)
        }

        // The anchors, written so that they survive whatever `RegExp` thinks a line is.
        if (c === "A") return two(inClass ? reCodePoint("A") : "(?<![\\s\\S])")
        if (c === "z") return two(inClass ? reCodePoint("z") : "(?![\\s\\S])")
        if (c === "Z") return two(inClass ? reCodePoint("Z") : "(?=\\n?(?![\\s\\S]))")
        if (c === "N") {
            if (inClass) reGone("\\N in a character class", "PCRE2 does not allow it there either")

            return two("[^\\n]")
        }

        // The two characters PCRE2 names and `RegExp` reads as a letter.
        if (c === "a") return two("\\x07")
        if (c === "e") return two("\\x1B")

        // `\Q ... \E`, which is everything between them as itself.
        if (c === "Q") {
            let j = at + 2
            let text = ""

            while (j < src.length && !(src[j] === "\\" && src[j + 1] === "E")) text += src[j++]

            return { text: reQuoted(text), end: j < src.length ? j + 2 : j }
        }

        if (c === "E") return two("")

        if (c === "K")
            reGone("\\K", "resetting where a match started is PCRE2's and `RegExp` has nothing that means it")

        if (c === "G")
            reGone("\\G", "an anchor at the end of the previous match is PCRE2's and `RegExp` has no such anchor")

        if (c === "C")
            reGone("\\C", "a single byte of a character is PCRE2's and a JavaScript string has no bytes to take")

        if (c === "X")
            reGone("\\X", "an extended grapheme cluster is PCRE2's and `RegExp` has nothing that means it")

        if (c === "g")
            reGone("\\g", "PCRE2's numbered and relative backreferences are written `\\1` and `\\k<name>` here, " +
                "and a relative one has no spelling at all")

        // `\p{...}` and `\P{...}`.
        if (c === "p" || c === "P") {
            if (src[at + 2] !== "{") reGone("\\" + c, "a one-letter property name has no spelling in `RegExp`")

            const close = src.indexOf("}", at + 3)

            if (close < 0) $.fault("this pattern has a `\\" + c + "{` that is never closed")

            return { text: unicodeProperty(c === "P", src.slice(at + 3, close), inClass), end: close + 1 }
        }

        // `\x{263A}` is `\u{263A}`; `\xHH` and a bare `\x` go through as they are written.
        if (c === "x" && src[at + 2] === "{") {
            const close = src.indexOf("}", at + 3)

            if (close < 0) $.fault("this pattern has a `\\x{` that is never closed")

            return { text: "\\u{" + src.slice(at + 3, close) + "}", end: close + 1 }
        }

        if (c === "x") {
            if (!/[0-9A-Fa-f]/.test(src[at + 2] ?? "")) return two("\\0")

            return { text: src.slice(at, at + 4), end: at + 4 }
        }

        // `\o{101}` is an octal code point, which `\u{...}` says in hexadecimal.
        if (c === "o") {
            if (src[at + 2] !== "{") reGone("\\o", "an octal escape is written `\\o{...}` in PCRE2 and not at all here")

            const close = src.indexOf("}", at + 3)

            if (close < 0) $.fault("this pattern has a `\\o{` that is never closed")

            const n = parseInt(src.slice(at + 3, close), 8)

            if (!Number.isFinite(n)) $.fault("this pattern has a `\\o{...}` that is not octal")

            return { text: "\\u{" + n.toString(16) + "}", end: close + 1 }
        }

        // `\k<n>`, `\k'n'` and `\k{n}` are one backreference written three ways; `RegExp` has the
        // first.
        if (c === "k" && (src[at + 2] === "'" || src[at + 2] === "{")) {
            const close = src.indexOf(src[at + 2] === "'" ? "'" : "}", at + 3)

            if (close < 0) $.fault("this pattern has a `\\k` that is never closed")

            return { text: "\\k<" + src.slice(at + 3, close) + ">", end: close + 1 }
        }

        // **A numbered backreference goes through and an octal escape does not.** PCRE2 reads
        // `\101` as a code point where no group 101 exists, which `RegExp` under `u` refuses; a
        // pattern meaning the character writes `\o{101}` and reaches the arm above.
        if (isDigitChar(c)) {
            if (c === "0") {
                if (isDigitChar(src[at + 2]))
                    reGone("\\0 followed by a digit", "an octal escape is written `\\o{...}` in PCRE2 and not at all here")

                return two("\\0")
            }

            let j = at + 1
            let digits = ""

            while (isDigitChar(src[j])) digits += src[j++]

            if (inClass)
                reGone("\\" + digits + " in a character class",
                    "a backreference cannot be a member of a class, and an octal escape has no spelling here")

            return { text: "\\" + digits, end: j }
        }

        // **An identity escape becomes the code point it names.** PCRE2 lets a backslash precede
        // any punctuation and `RegExp` under `u` allows it only before its own syntax characters,
        // so `\%` would be a syntax error where it is a literal `%`.
        if (!isWordChar(c)) {
            if ("^$\\.*+?()[]{}|/".indexOf(c) >= 0) return two("\\" + c)
            if (inClass && c === "-") return two("\\-")

            // **The code point and not the unit**, an escaped astral character being two of them.
            const point = src.codePointAt(at + 1)

            return { text: "\\u{" + point.toString(16) + "}", end: at + 1 + (point > 0xFFFF ? 2 : 1) }
        }

        // What is left is a letter both engines know -- `\d`, `\w`, `\b`, `\n`, `\cX` and the rest
        // -- and it goes through untouched.
        if (c === "c") return { text: src.slice(at, at + 3), end: at + 3 }

        return two("\\" + c)
    }

    // A character class, `[` to its matching `]`.
    const regexClass = (src, at, extended) => {
        let i = at + 1
        let out = "["

        if (src[i] === "^") {
            out += "^"
            i++
        }

        // **A `]` in the first position is a member and not the end**, which is PCRE2's rule and
        // not `RegExp`'s under `u`, so it is written out.
        if (src[i] === "]") {
            out += "\\]"
            i++
        }

        while (i < src.length && src[i] !== "]") {
            const c = src[i]

            if (c === "\\") {
                const r = regexEscape(src, i, true)

                out += r.text
                i = r.end
                continue
            }

            // `[:alpha:]` and `[:^alpha:]`, which are members of the class around them rather than
            // classes of their own.
            if (c === "[" && src[i + 1] === ":") {
                const close = src.indexOf(":]", i + 2)

                if (close < 0) $.fault("this pattern has a `[:` that is never closed")

                const negated = src[i + 2] === "^"
                const name = src.slice(i + (negated ? 3 : 2), close)
                const known = Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(POSIX_IN, name)
                const set = known ? (negated ? POSIX_OUT[name] : POSIX_IN[name]) : undefined

                if (set === undefined)
                    $.fault("`[:" + name + ":]` is not a POSIX class -- the classes are " +
                        Object.keys(POSIX_IN).join(", "))

                out += set
                i = close + 2
                continue
            }

            if (c === "[") {
                out += "\\["
                i++
                continue
            }

            out += c
            i += c.length
        }

        if (i >= src.length) $.fault("this pattern has a `[` that is never closed")

        return { text: out + "]", end: i + 1 }
    }

    // What follows a `(`.
    const regexGroup = (src, at) => {
        const plain = { text: "(", end: at + 1, lookbehind: false, opened: true }

        // **A backtracking verb is `(*` and nothing else starts that way**, so it is caught before
        // the `(?` forms rather than among them.
        if (src[at + 1] === "*") {
            const close = src.indexOf(")", at + 1)
            const verb = close < 0 ? "(*" : src.slice(at, close + 1)

            reGone(verb, "a backtracking control verb is PCRE2's and `RegExp` has nothing that means it")
        }

        if (src[at + 1] !== "?") return plain

        const c = src[at + 2]

        if (c === ":") return { text: "(?:", end: at + 3, lookbehind: false, opened: true }
        if (c === "=") return { text: "(?=", end: at + 3, lookbehind: false, opened: true }
        if (c === "!") return { text: "(?!", end: at + 3, lookbehind: false, opened: true }

        if (c === "<" && (src[at + 3] === "=" || src[at + 3] === "!"))
            return { text: "(?<" + src[at + 3], end: at + 4, lookbehind: true, opened: true }

        if (c === "<") return { text: "(?<", end: at + 3, lookbehind: false, opened: true }

        // `(?'name'` and `(?P<name>` are `(?<name>` written two other ways.
        if (c === "'") {
            const close = src.indexOf("'", at + 3)

            if (close < 0) $.fault("this pattern has a `(?'` that is never closed")

            return { text: "(?<" + src.slice(at + 3, close) + ">", end: close + 1, lookbehind: false,
                opened: true }
        }

        if (c === "P" && src[at + 3] === "<")
            return { text: "(?<", end: at + 4, lookbehind: false, opened: true }

        // **These two are WHOLE**, having eaten their own `)`: a named backreference is one atom
        // however PCRE2 spells it, and a comment leaves nothing behind at all.
        if (c === "P" && src[at + 3] === "=") {
            const close = src.indexOf(")", at + 4)

            if (close < 0) $.fault("this pattern has a `(?P=` that is never closed")

            return { text: "\\k<" + src.slice(at + 4, close) + ">", end: close + 1, lookbehind: false,
                opened: false }
        }

        if (c === "#") {
            const close = src.indexOf(")", at + 3)

            if (close < 0) $.fault("this pattern has a `(?#` that is never closed")

            return { text: "", end: close + 1, lookbehind: false, opened: false }
        }

        if (c === ">")
            reGone("(?>", "an atomic group is PCRE2's and `RegExp` has nothing that means it")

        if (c === "|")
            reGone("(?|", "a branch reset numbers two alternatives' groups alike, and `RegExp` numbers them in order")

        if (c === "(")
            reGone("(?(", "a conditional group is PCRE2's and `RegExp` has nothing that means it")

        if (c === "C")
            reGone("(?C", "a callout runs the host's own code and there is nothing here for it to run")

        if (c === "R" || c === "&" || isDigitChar(c) || c === "+" || c === "-" ||
            (c === "P" && src[at + 3] === ">"))
            reGone("(?" + c, "recursion into a pattern is PCRE2's and `RegExp` has nothing that means it")

        // **A scoped modifier goes through and a bare one does not.** `(?i:...)` is a group and
        // `RegExp` has it; `(?i)` changes the rest of the pattern from where it stands, which
        // `RegExp` cannot say -- and neither can be translated into the other.
        const close = src.indexOf(")", at + 2)
        const colon = src.indexOf(":", at + 2)

        if (colon >= 0 && (close < 0 || colon < close)) {
            const mods = src.slice(at + 2, colon)

            if (!/^[im-]*$/.test(mods))
                reGone("(?" + mods + ":",
                    "`RegExp` scopes only `i`, and `m` and `s` mean different things in the two engines")

            return { text: src.slice(at, colon + 1), end: colon + 1, lookbehind: false, opened: true }
        }

        reGone("(?" + (close < 0 ? "" : src.slice(at + 2, close)) + ")",
            "a modifier that runs to the end of the pattern is PCRE2's and `RegExp` has nothing that means it")
    }

    // -- the table -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

    // The flags a program may write. **`regex.sysl`'s words, so a refusal reads the same on both
    // back ends** -- and `g` is refused by name because it is the one people will try.
    const regexOptions = (flags) => {
        for (const c of flags) {
            if (c === "g")
                $.fault("there is no `g` flag -- `replace` already replaces every match and " +
                    "`findAll` already finds every one")

            if ("imsx".indexOf(c) < 0)
                $.fault("`" + c + "` is not a regular expression flag -- the flags are `i`, `m`, `s` and `x`")
        }
    }

    // The slot for this pattern, compiling it the first time it is asked for.
    //
    // **Keyed by the pattern and its flags, so an identical pattern compiles once** -- which is
    // `regex.sysl`'s table and its reason: nothing is ever released, so a slot always names the
    // pattern it named and one number is the whole value.
    // **The VALUE is interned and not only the slot**, which is what makes `regex("a") ==
    // regex("a")` true here as it is there. The interpreter's `RegexVal` is one number, so two of
    // them naming one slot are the same value by construction; a `new SRegex` each time would be
    // two objects and equality would fall through to comparing addresses.
    const regexSlot = (source, flags) => {
        const key = flags + ":" + source
        const found = REGEX_IDS.get(key)

        if (found !== undefined) return found

        regexOptions(flags)

        // **`g` is always set and `m` and `s` never are.** The walk that finds every match needs a
        // pattern it can start anywhere, and the other two have been translated away above.
        const translated = regexTranslate(source, flags)
        let re = null

        try {
            re = new RegExp(translated, flags.indexOf("i") >= 0 ? "gui" : "gu")
        } catch (e) {
            $.fault("this is not a regular expression: " + e.message)
        }

        const made = new SRegex(REGEX_TABLE.length)

        REGEX_TABLE.push({ source: source, flags: flags, re: re })
        REGEX_IDS.set(key, made)

        return made
    }

    // **Shown as it was written, between slashes**, which is `render.sysl`'s rendering: what a
    // reader is shown is what they wrote, not what node was given.
    const regexText = (v) => "/" + REGEX_TABLE[v.id].source + "/" + REGEX_TABLE[v.id].flags

    // Its own letter as a table key, for the reason every other kind has one: two values holding
    // the same number must not collide.
    const regexKey = (v) => (v instanceof SRegex ? "R" + v.id : null)
    """
